ith ...Bxe5 or attack it with ...f6.  \n4. **Q: d1-e2** – Develops queen, prepares potential castling queenside or connecting rooks, but it blocks the bishop on c1.  \n\nReason:  \nThe highest priority is to safeguard the king and mobilize our rooks. Castling kingside (K: e1-g1) accomplishes both and is almost always correct in the opening when available. It also removes the king from potential future pins along the e-file. Given Black’s queen on b8, staying in the center with the king is riskier. While d2-d4 is aggressive and fights for the center, castling first is more solid and prepares for a later central break.  \nNf3-e5 is tempting but premature — Black can simply trade and we lose a developed knight. K: e1-g1 is the safest and most standard move here, improving our position without creating new weaknesses.\n\nAnswer: K: e1-g1"}
