ng and pawn vs king endgames, the key is to use the king to support the pawn’s advance while preventing the enemy king from capturing or blocking. Here, black king is on h5, close to the pawn. If I push the pawn two squares (h2-h4), black king can immediately move to h6 or g6, then to h5/g5, and possibly capture the pawn if unsupported. So I should first bring my king closer to support the pawn.  \nLooking at valid moves:  \n- K: g2-h3 is valid. This puts my king on h3, directly in front of the pawn’s path (if pawn moves to h3, it’s blocked). But pawn hasn’t moved, so I can move king to h3, then next move pawn can move to h4 (since king is then on h3, pawn can’t move to h3). That’s okay: king on h3 protects h4, so pawn can move to h4 safely next turn, and black king can’t capture because it’s protected.  \nBut black king on h5 is adjacent to h4, so if I play K: g2-h3, black king can move to h6 (keeping distance) or maybe g5, and still contest h4. Still, with king on h3, the pawn’s advance to h4 is protected.  \nAlternatively, K: g2-f3 is more flexible, heading toward the center to cut off black king’s approach from the f-file. But f3 is farther from h4.  \nGiven the immediate need, K: g2-h3 seems solid—it guards h4 and positions the king to escort the pawn. Then next move, P: h2-h4 (or if black king moves, maybe h2-h3 depending).  \nI think K: g2-h3 is the best preparatory move.\n\nAnswer: K: g2-h3'}
