 pawn endgames, activity of the king is paramount. Currently Black's King is slightly more active (f6). White's King should move toward the center and toward the pawn to support its advance and also be ready to attack Black's potential counterplay.  \n**K: g2-f3** seems the most flexible: it centralizes the King, prepares to meet ...Kf5 with Ke3 or to go to g4 supporting h4 if needed later, and avoids committing the pawn prematurely. After Kf3, Black might play ...Ke5, then White can consider h4 followed by Kg4.  \nAdvancing the pawn immediately (h2-h4) may allow Black's King to attack it immediately with ...Kf5, forcing White's King to stay defensive. Therefore, improving King position first is better.\n\nAnswer: K: g2-f3"}
