\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[K: g5-f4, K: g5-f5, K: g5-f6, K: g5-g4, K: g5-g6, K: g5-h5, K: g5-h6, P: h4-h5]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: Very simplified endgame – White has a king (KWF1 at g5) and a pawn (PWF1 at h4). Black only has a king (KBF1 at g8). My pawn is advanced (h4) and can promote if it reaches h8. White to move. My valid moves include king moves around g5 and advancing the pawn to h5.\n\nEvaluation: Since the pawn is the key to winning (conversion to queen), I should push it. However, I must also use my king to support the pawn's advance and to block the black king from interfering. Right now, the pawn can move to h5, which is one step forward. After h5, it’s closer to promotion and also controls g6 (limiting black king’s access). My king currently at g5 can also move, but moving it might leave the pawn unsupported.  \n\nPromising moves:  \n1. **P: h4-h5** – Immediately advances the pawn, getting it closer to queening. This also fixes the pawn on a light square (h5), and my king is on a dark square (g5) so they are on different colors, which can be good for controlling adjacent squares. However, if I push the pawn without king support, black king might come to g7/h7 and try to capture it later.  \n2. **K: g5-f6** – Centralizes king and cuts off black king’s approach via f7 and e7. This could be more active king support before pushing pawn. But pawn stays on h4, one move slower.  \n3. **K: g5-h6** – Gets king in front of pawn, which might help escorting it, but that can sometimes block the pawn (though not here since pawn is on h4).  \n\nGiven that we are in a king and pawn vs king endgame, the principle is: advance the pawn while using your king to support it and to cut off the opponent’s king. The pawn is already on h4; pushing it immediately to h5 is natural. Black king is far away on g8, so it will take at least 3 moves to get to h7 (g8-f7-g6-h7 or g8-f7-f6-g5-h6-h7). Meanwhile, after h5, my king can move to h6 or g6 next turn to further support the pawn’s advance. If black king approaches, my king can intercept. This seems like a straightforward winning plan – push the pawn.\n\nReason: In king and pawn endgames, especially with a rook pawn, timing is important. The pawn is already on h4, so pushing it to h5 immediately gains space and reduces the number of moves needed to queen. With my king at g5, I can later move to g6 or h6 to support h6/h7. Since black king is at g8, it cannot immediately stop the pawn. The move P: h4-h5 is the most direct path to promotion and gives white the best chance to queen and win.\n\nAnswer: P: h4-h5"}
