\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[K: g5-f4, K: g5-f5, K: g5-f6, K: g5-g4, K: g5-g6, K: g5-h4, K: g5-h6, P: h5-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "**Situation:**  \nThe board is extremely sparse. White has a king at g5 and a pawn at h5. Black has only a king at h8 (flagged KBF1, meaning it has moved). My king is relatively centralized, while the black king is cornered. My pawn is on h5, one step from promotion. However, moving the pawn immediately to h6 might not be best because the black king is far away and may not be able to stop promotion if I first improve my king's position.\n\n**Evaluation:**  \n1. **P: h5-h6** – Moves pawn closer to promotion, but black king can approach (Kg8, Kf7) to blockade. Without my king supporting, promotion may be difficult.  \n2. **K: g5-f6** – Moves king toward center and closer to pawn/black king, cutting off black king’s approach (e.g., after h6, black king can’t easily reach g7).  \n3. **K: g5-g6** – Similar idea, but f6 seems more active in controlling key squares near promotion path.  \n4. Other king moves (f4, g4, h4, f5, h5) either retreat or don’t make progress toward helping the pawn queen.\n\n**Reason:**  \nIn king and pawn endgames, the key is to use the king to support the pawn’s promotion while preventing the opponent’s king from stopping it. Right now, the black king is on h8. If I play **K: g5-f6**, I control e7, g7, and f7, making it hard for black’s king to get near my pawn after h6. Black’s only move after that likely is Kg8, but then I can play h6 and my king covers g7, so black can’t take the pawn. This should force promotion and win.  \nMoving the pawn immediately (h5-h6) allows black Kg8 next, and then if I play Kf6, black Kf7 opposes my king and may draw. So bringing the king first is a standard winning technique in such endings.\n\n**Answer:** K: g5-f6"}
