\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[K: f6-e5, K: f6-e6, K: f6-e7, K: f6-f5, K: f6-f7, K: f6-g5, P: h5-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "**Situation:**  \nThe board is almost empty except for:  \n- White king at f6 (KWF1, has moved)  \n- White pawn at h5 (PWF1, has moved)  \n- Black king at h7 (KBF1, has moved)  \n- No other pieces, so it's a king and pawn endgame.  \nWhite’s pawn is on the h-file, already advanced to the 5th rank. Black’s king is on h7, blocking the pawn’s direct advance for now.  \n\n**Evaluation:**  \nKey factors:  \n1. White’s king is more active (f6) than Black’s king (h7), but not yet dominating the promotion square.  \n2. The pawn can push h5–h6, but then it will be directly opposed by the black king on h7 — this usually leads to a blockade unless the white king can come and chase the black king away.  \n3. In king and pawn vs king endgames, the main winning idea is to get your king in front of your pawn and escort it to promotion, or to cut off the opponent’s king.  \n4. Currently, if we push h5–h6, Black king stays on h7, white king can try to approach, but black will just shuffle between h7 and g8/g7, likely a draw if white king can’t force opposition.  \n5. Better plan: use white king to gain opposition and squeeze black king away from h7 before pushing pawn.  \n\nPromising moves from the list:  \n- **K: f6-e7**: Moves toward black king, threatens to take control of g7/g8 squares, cuts black king’s escape to center.  \n- **K: f6-f7**: Even more aggressive, directly steps next to black king (opposition), but black king can only go to g8 or h8, possibly allowing white to then push pawn.  \n- **K: f6-g5**: Not as direct — moves king sideways away from pawn’s promotion path, not ideal.  \n- **P: h5-h6**: Premature; after …Kg8, white king f6–g6 maybe, but black Kf8/h8 can hold draw unless white can triangulate. With pawn on h6, black king on g8, opposition is trickier.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nThe move **K: f6-f7** puts the white king directly in front of the pawn’s promotion path and gives direct opposition to black king (white Kf7 vs black Kh7 is one rank apart, horizontally). From f7, if black moves Kh8, white can play Kg6 (next turn) threatening Kh6 and pawn promotion, forcing black Kg8 then white can maybe push pawn. If black moves Kg8 after Kf7, then white Ke7/Kg6 options come.  \nThis is the most forcing and retains flexibility: after Kf7, black’s only moves are Kh8 or Kg8. White can respond with Kg6 or Ke7 accordingly, improving king position to escort pawn.  \n\nPushing the pawn immediately (h5-h6) lessens winning chances because it fixes the pawn on h6, allowing black king to stay close to promotion square without being displaced.  \n\n**Answer:** K: f6-f7"}
